     KSP Resolver.kt KDoc (google/ksp main); Android "Migrate from kapt to KSP". -->

<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">After a migration from kapt, the build fails with <code>Unresolved reference: UserJsonAdapter</code>. Yesterday that class existed. Nobody wrote it by hand: a processor generated it. Now somebody has to work out which step stopped producing it.</p>

KSP lets a processor read your Kotlin declarations at build time and write new source files. Those files are then compiled with the rest of the module. This chapter covers why that beats kapt's Java stubs, how to write a processor, and how incremental processing decides what to rerun. The additions here split code generation into steps you can check one at a time, so a missing class has a clear place to look.

<!-- @diagram 983b8acc34b1133f -->
### From annotation to compiled class

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-flow"><div class="node"><strong>Your <code>.kt</code> sources</strong></div><div class="node"><strong>Kotlin compiler</strong><small>parses the module</small></div><div class="node"><strong>KSP Resolver</strong><small>the symbol graph</small></div><div class="node is-key"><strong>Your processor</strong></div><div class="node"><strong>Generated <code>.kt</code> files</strong></div><div class="node" data-state="done"><strong>Final compilation</strong></div></div><figcaption>Redrawn from the original diagram. Generated code is ordinary Kotlin source: it is compiled, packaged and shrunk like code you wrote.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after How KSP Sees Your Code :: animation -->
### Where did the generated class go?

